  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 337 AM MDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nRain chances continue into Saturday morning as southwesterly flow \naloft continues to eject embedded shortwaves across the area. \nProbability of Precipitation (PoPs) are around 30% for the northeast \ncounty warning area (CWA) through Saturday evening. High \ntemperatures are forecast in the 60s with lows in the 40s, so rain \nis the expected precipitation type for any showers that are able to \nform. We could see some fog as dewpoints are forecast in the 50s. \nSoutheasterly surface winds could advect additional moisture into \nthe area, increasing chances for fog. \n\nFor Sunday, high temperatures are forecast in the 50s to 60s. Storm \nchances start Sunday evening with PoPs of 50-75% increasing west to \neast. Precipitation type will likely be rain based on forecast \ntemperatures and lack of instability in the environment. \n\nUpper-level features are likely to send us into a more active \nweather pattern Sunday evening through at least Tuesday. A deep \ntrough is forecast to extend from the Pacific Northwest (PNW) \nsoutheast with a second trough not too far behind it in the western \nPacific. As these features propogate east, it will allow the \nsubtropical jet to drift north placing a strong jet streak across \nthe region Sunday. Models also show a surface low set up in eastern \nColorado and propogate east sometime Sunday evening or Monday. \nEnsembles show the surface low setting up somewhere in the Central \nPlains or the Oklahoma/Texas Panhandle. The interaction, track and \nevolution of these surface and upper-level feathers will play a \nmajor role in our weather pattern heading into the work week. \nCurrently, models diverge on how these interactions will play out. \n\nThe upper-level pattern could support a high wind event Monday \nand Tuesday, but the big question will be where the surface low \ntracks and the amount of cloud cover across the CWA. Clear skies\nwill allow stronger winds to mix down to the surface while \nclouds will keep the winds above the surface. If we end up with \nclear skies and a low further south or east of the CWA, we could\nsee wind gusts up to 60 mph Monday and Tuesday afternoon. Dust \nwould be unlikely for areas that received precipitation over the\nweekend and current forecast lapse rates are unsupportive of \ndust being lofted. A low tracking over the CWA would likely \nincrease precipitation chances and cloud cover, overall lowering\nrisk for strong winds. Low confidence in which scenario will \nplay out. It will heavily depend on the track and evolution of \nthe aforementioned upper-level troughs over the weekend.",
